#!/usr/bin/python3
"""The web UI of Server OPS (repository setup-server): the worker's status and the bin/ commands, on 127.0.0.1 only.

bin/install-ui installs this as /usr/local/sbin/setup-server-ui and runs it as the systemd service
setup-server-ui of its own account, setup-ui (not setup: pipeline steps run as setup and must not reach
it). It listens on 127.0.0.1 only (UI_PORT, default 9102); from a laptop: ssh -L 9102:127.0.0.1:9102 server.

Access. Users and groups live in $STATE_DIRECTORY/accounts.json (class Accounts): a user belongs to groups, a
group gives each section "view" or "manage" (its actions too). The first start makes the ui.env
login (UI_USER, UI_PASSWORD_HASH) the first administrator; a new ui.env password (sudo bin/install-ui
--new-password) gives it back later. A browser logs in on /login and gets a session cookie (HttpOnly,
SameSite=Lax; only its sha256 is stored, sessions.json); a script may send Basic auth of any enabled user.
Passwords are PBKDF2, compared in constant time; wrong logins from one client are rate-limited. Every request
needs a Host of 127.0.0.1/localhost (a DNS-rebound name is refused). Every POST also needs the CSRF token of its
form: an HMAC of the user with a key made at start (a restart invalidates the open forms) and, when the browser
sends one, an Origin of 127.0.0.1/localhost.

The API for agents. /api/v1 answers GETs with JSON (the index lists every endpoint; /api/v1/openapi.json): the
deploys, backups, pipelines, pull requests and alarms of the same status the pages show, never a secret. A caller
sends an API token (Authorization: Bearer ops_…; made on the Users page, shown once, only its sha256 kept in
accounts.json) or Basic auth of a user; the token's groups decide what it reads, as a user's do. No cookie counts
there, nothing is changed through it (any other method: 405), and every request is a line of
$STATE_DIRECTORY/api-access.log (never the token).

What it runs. Status: `sudo -n $LIBRARY/bin/server-status` (read-only, JSON: the overview, the logs, a project
container's docker logs and the worker's /metrics). Actions: the root-owned copies
in $LIBRARY/bin (never a writable checkout) through sudo, and bin/update of the admin clone as its owner;
the sudo rule of bin/install-ui allows nothing else. Arguments are checked here against strict patterns and
always passed as an argv list. Every action first runs with --check; only that previewed command (kept
in memory, single use) runs on "Run". A run is a detached job (output in $STATE_DIRECTORY/jobs, polled
by its page) that outlives a restart of this service: bin/update refreshes this program while it runs.
Deploys, "backup now" and the runner test start Bitbucket custom pipelines through the API (history stays
in Bitbucket), with BITBUCKET_ACCESS_TOKEN_<NAME> or BITBUCKET_ACCESS_TOKEN; redirects are not followed
and the token is never printed or logged. Every action — time, user, client, argv, exit code; never stdin
or a token — is appended to $SETUP_SERVER_ROOT/ui-actions.log; without that log nothing runs.

Usage: setup-server-ui            serve (settings from the environment, see bin/install-ui)
       setup-server-ui --check    validate the settings and show where it would listen
       setup-server-ui --hash     read a password on stdin and print its UI_PASSWORD_HASH (bin/install-ui)
       setup-server-ui --job DIR  run one action (internal: the UI starts it detached)
"""

